1
00:00:36,953 --> 00:00:39,330
- Hey, Jane.
- Got a positive I.D. on the victim.

2
00:00:39,455 --> 00:00:42,750
Viktor Mendelssohn, 62.
He was a diamond cutter.

3
00:00:42,959 --> 00:00:45,294
He has a shop
in the San Francisco Diamond district.

4
00:00:45,795 --> 00:00:48,756
Same as it ever was...
more bodies, more death.

5
00:00:48,881 --> 00:00:50,007
Not exactly the same.

6
00:00:50,132 --> 00:00:52,468
This one was eviscerated
with an electric rotary saw.

7
00:00:53,010 --> 00:00:55,263
Excuse me.
Your toe.

8
00:00:57,181 --> 00:00:58,307
Careful.

9
00:00:59,892 --> 00:01:01,102
Sorry.

10
00:01:05,648 --> 00:01:07,650
Is it me, or does Jane seem
a little bit off

11
00:01:07,775 --> 00:01:09,860
since the feds took Lorelei?

12
00:01:10,236 --> 00:01:12,363
Well, he spent six months
setting a trap for Red John.

13
00:01:12,822 --> 00:01:15,491
He has nothing to show for it.
How would you feel?

14
00:01:16,909 --> 00:01:18,869
Maybe we should invite him out
for a night with the boys.

15
00:01:20,538 --> 00:01:21,789
All right, you ask him.

16
00:01:44,478 --> 00:01:47,732
Well, I-I came out this morning
to snip my herbs.

17
00:01:47,857 --> 00:01:49,400
It's always best to do that

18
00:01:49,525 --> 00:01:51,485
right after
the morning dew evaporates.

19
00:01:51,611 --> 00:01:55,031
And when I didn't see Viktor
I got a really bad feeling.

20
00:01:55,448 --> 00:01:57,074
Do you usually see him
in the morning?

21
00:01:57,199 --> 00:01:59,869
Every morning.
We chat over the fence.

22
00:02:00,119 --> 00:02:02,580
Hey. Jane, this is Betty Fulford.

23
00:02:02,705 --> 00:02:03,748
- Hey.
- She lives next door.

24
00:02:03,873 --> 00:02:04,957
Patrick Jane.

25
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,544
So did Viktor tell you about anybody
who might wanna hurt him?

26
00:02:08,669 --> 00:02:09,795
No!

27
00:02:11,047 --> 00:02:14,717
Although... last night
when I was getting ready for bed

28
00:02:14,842 --> 00:02:18,596
I did hear him arguing
with a man in his house.

29
00:02:18,721 --> 00:02:21,098
- What were they arguing about?
- Flowers.

30
00:02:21,265 --> 00:02:22,308
Really?

31
00:02:22,433 --> 00:02:24,560
Blue orchids, to be exact.

32
00:02:24,685 --> 00:02:27,480
They... they were shouting,
and Viktor never shouts.

33
00:02:27,605 --> 00:02:29,106
And you were in your bedroom
at this time?

34
00:02:29,231 --> 00:02:31,400
That's right. That's right.
My window was open.

35
00:02:32,318 --> 00:02:34,820
Did you happen to catch a glimpse
of the other man?

36
00:02:34,987 --> 00:02:37,323
Well, I tried to snoop, but I couldn't
see into the kitchen.

37
00:02:37,448 --> 00:02:39,492
That's where they were
having it out.

38
00:02:40,785 --> 00:02:42,286
What's that noise?

39
00:02:43,079 --> 00:02:45,748
I have absolutely no idea,
but I will investigate.

40
00:04:56,754 --> 00:04:59,423
- Everything okay in there?
- "Everything's fine!"

41
00:05:00,674 --> 00:05:02,426
It's all peachy.

42
00:05:10,184 --> 00:05:12,061
What are you doing in there?

43
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:37,025
Rabbit stew.
Yummy.

44
00:05:38,110 --> 00:05:40,028
We need to fatten you up.

45
00:05:40,195 --> 00:05:41,447
Come on, fella.

46
00:05:42,156 --> 00:05:43,323
Hello.

47
00:05:49,496 --> 00:05:51,165
{\an8}What time did you get
to the house this morning?

48
00:05:51,331 --> 00:05:53,250
{\an8}Same time every Monday.
8:30.

49
00:05:53,375 --> 00:05:54,793
I walk from the bus stop.

50
00:05:54,918 --> 00:05:55,961
Do you have a key
to the house?

51
00:05:56,128 --> 00:05:58,422
No. Mr. Mendelssohn
let me in.

52
00:05:58,547 --> 00:05:59,965
But this morning

53
00:06:00,716 --> 00:06:04,261
{\an8}he didn't answer the door,
so I looked in the window.

54
00:06:04,428 --> 00:06:06,472
Well, you did the right thing.

55
00:06:06,972 --> 00:06:09,892
Calling the police...
very brave. Very brave.

56
00:06:10,100 --> 00:06:13,270
{\an8}I told Mr. Mendelssohn
something bad was coming

57
00:06:13,395 --> 00:06:15,647
{\an8}but he didn't listen to me.

58
00:06:15,856 --> 00:06:18,358
Why did you think something bad
was on its way?

59
00:06:18,484 --> 00:06:20,068
{\an8}The black ants.

60
00:06:20,569 --> 00:06:22,154
{\an8}For two weeks I come now

61
00:06:22,279 --> 00:06:25,157
{\an8}and I clean black ants
from the house.

62
00:06:25,324 --> 00:06:26,366
Black ants?

63
00:06:26,492 --> 00:06:27,826
{\an8}In some South American countries

64
00:06:27,951 --> 00:06:30,829
{\an8}it's said that if black ants
enter your house

65
00:06:30,954 --> 00:06:33,040
{\an8}then someone in your family
might die.

66
00:06:34,124 --> 00:06:36,835
{\an8}- You understand.
- "Si."

67
00:06:37,211 --> 00:06:40,130
This is... an eagle's claw.

68
00:06:40,672 --> 00:06:43,050
It will help you find
who did this.

69
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:44,802
Take it, please.

70
00:06:44,927 --> 00:06:47,221
{\an8}Okay. Thank you.
I will. I'll just...

71
00:06:48,222 --> 00:06:50,891
Okay. Thank you.
Ooh. Oops. Excuse me.

72
00:06:55,145 --> 00:06:57,898
{\an8}Bunny?
Where's the bunny?

73
00:07:01,944 --> 00:07:04,029
{\an8}Here. Rigsby, hold this.

74
00:07:05,405 --> 00:07:07,407
{\an8}Has anyone seen a rabbit
around here?

75
00:07:27,469 --> 00:07:29,972
{\an8}Excuse me. Uh, did you happen
to see a rabbit?

76
00:07:30,097 --> 00:07:31,306
Yeah.

77
00:07:31,974 --> 00:07:33,183
Thank you.

78
00:08:04,590 --> 00:08:06,091
{\an8}Hello, fella.

79
00:08:09,845 --> 00:08:11,013
Yeah?

80
00:08:11,972 --> 00:08:13,140
There...

81
00:08:13,265 --> 00:08:15,684
Ahh, there you are. Huh?

82
00:08:16,351 --> 00:08:17,644
Gotcha.

83
00:08:19,187 --> 00:08:20,397
You found him.

84
00:08:21,982 --> 00:08:23,817
I take it this belongs to you.

85
00:08:24,443 --> 00:08:26,486
Yeah. It's the third time
he's escaped.

86
00:08:26,653 --> 00:08:28,822
Chews through that metal
like a floppy-eared rat.

87
00:08:28,947 --> 00:08:30,949
- Thanks for finding him.
- Oh.

88
00:08:31,241 --> 00:08:32,492
Pleasure.

89
00:08:33,535 --> 00:08:35,287
Certainly took your time
getting here.

90
00:08:36,455 --> 00:08:38,165
You were expecting me?

91
00:08:38,290 --> 00:08:39,333
Yeah, of course.

92
00:08:41,835 --> 00:08:43,420
You know something
about the murder?

93
00:08:44,338 --> 00:08:46,506
Yeah.
Poor old Mr. Mendelssohn.

94
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:48,675
You know anything
that could help us?

95
00:08:49,468 --> 00:08:50,719
You tell me.

96
00:08:51,386 --> 00:08:53,221
How can I know what you know?

97
00:08:53,639 --> 00:08:55,724
How can I know what I know
will help you?

98
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:58,936
You're testing me.

99
00:08:59,061 --> 00:09:00,228
Why?

100
00:09:01,021 --> 00:09:03,941
You don't have issues
with authority figures.

101
00:09:04,566 --> 00:09:07,194
You know you're the smartest person
in most rooms.

102
00:09:08,153 --> 00:09:09,821
What's your name?

103
00:09:09,947 --> 00:09:11,782
Am I required to tell you?

104
00:09:11,907 --> 00:09:14,868
Well, I'm just a consultant.
You don't have to tell me anything.

105
00:09:14,993 --> 00:09:16,161
Then I won't.

106
00:09:16,328 --> 00:09:18,413
Okay. Have it your way.

107
00:09:19,414 --> 00:09:21,083
- Wait. Where are you going?
- Well, you're playing me.

108
00:09:21,208 --> 00:09:22,334
You don't have anything helpful
to offer me.

109
00:09:22,459 --> 00:09:23,752
Stop.

110
00:09:29,257 --> 00:09:30,509
Who's playing who?

111
00:09:31,718 --> 00:09:34,179
You think you can manipulate me
with reverse psychology?

112
00:09:34,304 --> 00:09:35,806
Seems to be working.

113
00:09:36,723 --> 00:09:38,308
Well, it's not.

114
00:09:41,353 --> 00:09:42,980
I'll help you.

115
00:09:43,146 --> 00:09:45,649
But not because of your lame attempt
to prey on my ego.

116
00:09:45,774 --> 00:09:47,192
I'm bored.

117
00:09:47,567 --> 00:09:49,945
Not a whole lot's going on
in my world.

118
00:09:50,070 --> 00:09:51,571
And what world's that?

119
00:09:52,781 --> 00:09:54,157
Come on.

120
00:10:08,672 --> 00:10:11,216
Mr. Mendelssohn hardly went to
his store in the diamond district.

121
00:10:11,341 --> 00:10:12,426
He, um...

122
00:10:12,551 --> 00:10:14,386
liked to work close
to home here.

123
00:10:14,553 --> 00:10:16,096
This is his workshop.

124
00:10:17,723 --> 00:10:19,474
Have you been inside before?

125
00:10:19,599 --> 00:10:21,935
No. But I've seen him go in a lot.

126
00:10:22,060 --> 00:10:23,478
He spent all of his time here.

127
00:10:26,732 --> 00:10:27,941
Wow.

128
00:10:29,484 --> 00:10:31,153
This place is so cool.

129
00:10:34,197 --> 00:10:37,284
Weird... is what it is.

130
00:10:41,830 --> 00:10:43,707
It's like a place in a dream.

131
00:10:44,875 --> 00:10:47,502
Close, but no cigar.

132
00:10:47,627 --> 00:10:49,046
You're getting warmer, though.

133
00:10:51,089 --> 00:10:52,382
What?

134
00:10:57,846 --> 00:10:59,347
Who are you?

135
00:10:59,514 --> 00:11:01,058
What's your name?

136
00:11:02,059 --> 00:11:03,935
Here we go again
with the name.

137
00:11:05,103 --> 00:11:06,563
You have a definite tendency
to obsess.

138
00:11:06,688 --> 00:11:08,732
- Do people ever tell you that?
- All the time.

139
00:11:10,692 --> 00:11:12,360
What are you hiding?

140
00:11:13,028 --> 00:11:15,238
You tell me.
You're the mentalist.

141
00:11:15,363 --> 00:11:17,240
I never said I was a mentalist.

142
00:11:17,657 --> 00:11:20,285
Spooky.
So how did I know?

143
00:11:21,995 --> 00:11:25,165
You're playing a little game with me
and I wanna know why.

144
00:11:26,166 --> 00:11:27,959
Who are you?
What's your name?

145
00:11:29,544 --> 00:11:31,880
- Charlotte.
- Okay. Well, that's half a name.

146
00:11:32,005 --> 00:11:33,507
It's a start.

147
00:11:34,007 --> 00:11:35,759
Half a nice name.

148
00:11:36,802 --> 00:11:38,512
I had a daughter
named Charlotte.

149
00:11:40,722 --> 00:11:42,307
I know.

150
00:11:43,433 --> 00:11:44,726
No.

151
00:11:45,560 --> 00:11:47,145
No, you don't.

152
00:11:48,105 --> 00:11:49,981
Who put you up to this?
Red John?

153
00:11:50,107 --> 00:11:51,942
Red John, Red John, Red John.

154
00:11:52,109 --> 00:11:54,194
I am so over Red John.

155
00:11:54,986 --> 00:11:56,696
You're not my daughter.

156
00:11:58,281 --> 00:12:00,033
My daughter was murdered.

157
00:12:01,910 --> 00:12:03,620
I found her body.

158
00:12:08,542 --> 00:12:11,253
"You are safe, you are loved"

159
00:12:11,378 --> 00:12:12,963
"and you are wise."

160
00:12:14,214 --> 00:12:16,049
Who told you those words?

161
00:12:16,883 --> 00:12:18,426
You did.

162
00:12:18,677 --> 00:12:20,762
Every night
when you tucked me into bed.

163
00:12:24,891 --> 00:12:28,061
Hey, Jane. How'd you find this place
before we did?

164
00:12:28,770 --> 00:12:31,773
Lisbon, good. Uh, could you run
a background check on this girl?

165
00:12:31,940 --> 00:12:35,360
She's playing a con on me.
A very good one.

166
00:12:37,028 --> 00:12:38,780
And who are you, miss?

167
00:12:39,406 --> 00:12:41,908
Hello. I'm his daughter...
Charlotte.

168
00:12:42,033 --> 00:12:44,035
I've heard so much about you.
It's nice to meet you.

169
00:12:44,161 --> 00:12:45,912
My daughter's dead!

170
00:12:48,498 --> 00:12:50,917
- He's having another episode.
- Yeah.

171
00:12:51,668 --> 00:12:54,754
Come on, buddy.
Why don't you have a seat?

172
00:12:58,508 --> 00:13:00,510
- What is wrong with you?
- It's all right.

173
00:13:00,635 --> 00:13:01,887
We'll explain it all to you.

174
00:13:02,012 --> 00:13:04,097
We'll tell you everything.
Just sit down first.

175
00:13:05,974 --> 00:13:07,434
No.

176
00:13:07,559 --> 00:13:09,352
Charlotte's dead.

177
00:13:25,994 --> 00:13:27,537
Everything okay in there?

178
00:13:28,371 --> 00:13:29,831
What's going on?

179
00:13:31,708 --> 00:13:33,043
Oh, my God!

180
00:13:42,899 --> 00:13:46,486
- Did he ingest anything?
- Uh, tea. He... he drank hot tea.

181
00:13:46,611 --> 00:13:48,864
- Hey.
- His heartbeat's irregular.

182
00:13:49,448 --> 00:13:51,408
- Hey.
- Pupils dilated.

183
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:54,828
- Skin's unusually dry.
- Right here.

184
00:13:55,662 --> 00:13:56,705
Get that to the lab!

185
00:13:56,830 --> 00:13:59,207
In fact, extend the primary
crime scene into the kitchen.

186
00:13:59,791 --> 00:14:01,168
No need for the lab.

187
00:14:01,293 --> 00:14:04,629
It's belladonna, the devil's cherry.
I know it from working narcotics.

188
00:14:04,755 --> 00:14:06,173
Belladonna's highly toxic.

189
00:14:06,298 --> 00:14:07,841
That's why users dilute it
with tea.

190
00:14:07,966 --> 00:14:09,092
The berries have
a sweet flavor.

191
00:14:09,217 --> 00:14:11,136
They say it gives you
instant hallucinations.

192
00:14:11,303 --> 00:14:13,972
Well, if the victim was tripping
it would make him a lot easier to kill.

193
00:14:14,264 --> 00:14:16,725
- Is he gonna be okay?
- He's holding his own.

194
00:14:16,933 --> 00:14:19,269
That's a good sign
but we need to get him to a hospital

195
00:14:19,394 --> 00:14:21,188
- and pump his stomach.
- I'm gonna go with Jane.

196
00:14:21,313 --> 00:14:23,982
Cho, you're in charge.
You two push on the investigation.

197
00:14:24,983 --> 00:14:26,109
It's okay.

198
00:14:28,528 --> 00:14:31,448
You know that Mendelssohn cut some
of the rarest stones of his time?

199
00:14:31,573 --> 00:14:33,158
He was considered a master.

200
00:14:33,450 --> 00:14:34,743
You have his client list?

201
00:14:34,868 --> 00:14:36,411
Yeah, you should see this thing.

202
00:14:36,536 --> 00:14:39,081
Leaders of industry, royalty,
Hollywood stars.

203
00:14:39,581 --> 00:14:41,792
- Yeah, we need to run them down.
- I'm all over it.

204
00:14:41,917 --> 00:14:43,460
I already have a call in to Clooney.

205
00:14:44,127 --> 00:14:45,879
Preliminary forensics report.

206
00:14:46,046 --> 00:14:48,548
There were no useable prints
on the teapot or bowl.

207
00:14:48,673 --> 00:14:50,467
Well, except for Jane's prints.

208
00:14:50,592 --> 00:14:52,594
Great. Smudging fingerprints,
drinking evidence...

209
00:14:52,719 --> 00:14:54,179
he's like a drunk uncle.

210
00:14:54,304 --> 00:14:56,473
What about the electric saw
that was used on the victim?

211
00:14:56,598 --> 00:14:58,725
Two sets of prints.
One belongs to Mendelssohn

212
00:14:58,850 --> 00:15:00,477
the other unidentified.

213
00:15:00,602 --> 00:15:02,562
M.E. puts time of death
around midnight.

214
00:15:02,813 --> 00:15:04,523
Now Lisbon said
the next-door neighbor

215
00:15:04,648 --> 00:15:07,359
heard Mendelssohn arguing
with a man in his kitchen last night.

216
00:15:07,609 --> 00:15:09,111
Did Mendelssohn live alone?

217
00:15:09,236 --> 00:15:11,405
He was sharing his home
with his daughter Melaina.

218
00:15:11,530 --> 00:15:13,532
Guess she hit hard times
last year and moved in.

219
00:15:13,782 --> 00:15:15,367
She writes children's books.

220
00:15:15,492 --> 00:15:16,993
- Where was she last night?
- Big Sur.

221
00:15:17,119 --> 00:15:18,453
Rented a cabin to work on a book.

222
00:15:18,578 --> 00:15:20,163
I notified her this morning.
She's driving home now.

223
00:15:20,288 --> 00:15:21,540
Yeah. Definitely meet
with the daughter.

224
00:15:21,665 --> 00:15:23,041
See if she knows who was
at the house last night.

225
00:15:23,166 --> 00:15:24,960
- Right.
- Did the victim have any coworkers?

226
00:15:25,085 --> 00:15:27,129
An assistant named
Julian Gallego.

227
00:15:27,254 --> 00:15:28,713
Bring him in.
Let me know what you find.

228
00:15:28,839 --> 00:15:29,881
Okay.

229
00:15:32,717 --> 00:15:34,136
It's all right. I got it.

230
00:15:35,429 --> 00:15:36,680
CBI.

231
00:15:37,264 --> 00:15:38,765
Oh, yeah. Mr. Clooney.

232
00:15:38,932 --> 00:15:41,184
Yeah, this is Agent Cho. I just have
a couple questions for you.

233
00:15:52,362 --> 00:15:53,822
This way. This way, please.

234
00:15:54,739 --> 00:15:56,533
- All right, we'll take it from here.
- Is he gonna be o...

235
00:15:59,911 --> 00:16:02,247
Viktor was like Stradivari.

236
00:16:02,372 --> 00:16:04,791
He could, uh, hear a flaw
in the diamond

237
00:16:04,916 --> 00:16:07,627
by the way his grinding wheel
changed its song.

238
00:16:08,170 --> 00:16:09,754
How long did you work
for him, Julian?

239
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:11,381
Four years.

240
00:16:11,506 --> 00:16:14,759
Who's going to teach me now?
There is no one like Viktor.

241
00:16:15,177 --> 00:16:16,928
When did you last see him?

242
00:16:17,053 --> 00:16:18,096
Yesterday.

243
00:16:18,221 --> 00:16:20,348
We spent the whole day
in the workshop together.

244
00:16:20,474 --> 00:16:23,268
He was showing me
how to open a window.

245
00:16:23,393 --> 00:16:24,728
A window?

246
00:16:27,647 --> 00:16:29,524
Before making the first cut

247
00:16:29,649 --> 00:16:32,319
Viktor would spend weeks
getting to know a rare gem.

248
00:16:32,444 --> 00:16:34,613
You have to, uh, polish a window

249
00:16:34,738 --> 00:16:36,198
in the cloudy surface
of the stone

250
00:16:36,323 --> 00:16:38,074
so that you can peer
into its heart.

251
00:16:38,533 --> 00:16:40,827
- Sounds romantic.
- It is.

252
00:16:41,119 --> 00:16:44,539
A careless cut can shatter
your treasure.

253
00:16:45,874 --> 00:16:48,877
Did your boss come into possession
of any rare gems lately?

254
00:16:49,002 --> 00:16:53,423
Oh, yes.
A 102-carat blue diamond.

255
00:16:54,132 --> 00:16:56,092
Most master cutters
will turn into old men

256
00:16:56,218 --> 00:16:59,387
and... and never touch
a diamond over, uh, 50 carats.

257
00:16:59,513 --> 00:17:02,474
100 carats? How much is something
like that worth?

258
00:17:02,933 --> 00:17:05,101
Well, it's just a chunk
of rough crystal now

259
00:17:05,227 --> 00:17:07,229
but after Viktor
was through with it...

260
00:17:08,313 --> 00:17:09,981
$20 million.

261
00:17:10,857 --> 00:17:13,401
And where is that diamond?

262
00:17:13,527 --> 00:17:14,819
Viktor took it home with him.

263
00:17:14,945 --> 00:17:17,239
He took all of his clients' stones
home with him at night.

264
00:17:17,864 --> 00:17:19,199
He kept them close

265
00:17:20,116 --> 00:17:21,493
in a...

266
00:17:21,618 --> 00:17:23,245
hidden shoulder wallet.

267
00:17:24,746 --> 00:17:27,707
There was no shoulder wallet
found on Viktor's body.

268
00:17:28,792 --> 00:17:30,794
Did anyone have it in
for your boss?

269
00:17:30,919 --> 00:17:32,963
Fahad. Fahad threatened him.

270
00:17:33,129 --> 00:17:35,465
- Who's that?
- Fahad Ranjani.

271
00:17:35,632 --> 00:17:37,884
Another diamond cutter.
Uh, minor talent.

272
00:17:38,176 --> 00:17:40,679
The Blue Orchid
is a career-making stone.

273
00:17:40,887 --> 00:17:42,889
Fahad thought it was going to him.

274
00:17:43,014 --> 00:17:44,349
The Blue Orchid?

275
00:17:45,058 --> 00:17:47,352
- Blue Orchid?
- That's right.

276
00:17:47,477 --> 00:17:49,938
When was your last contact
with your father, Melaina?

277
00:17:50,814 --> 00:17:54,025
Yesterday morning.
I-I called him from the cabin.

278
00:17:54,859 --> 00:17:56,945
And he didn't indicate
that anything was wrong?

279
00:17:57,362 --> 00:17:58,446
On the contrary.

280
00:17:58,572 --> 00:18:01,032
He was so excited
about that new stone.

281
00:18:01,157 --> 00:18:02,826
According to his
assistant Julian

282
00:18:02,993 --> 00:18:06,496
he liked to hide his clients' gems
on his body?

283
00:18:08,456 --> 00:18:11,293
Dad never took off
that beat-up shoulder pouch.

284
00:18:11,418 --> 00:18:12,961
Even in bed.

285
00:18:13,086 --> 00:18:14,671
Drove mom crazy.

286
00:18:14,921 --> 00:18:16,339
And why would he do that?

287
00:18:17,048 --> 00:18:19,676
He was raised not to trust
security services.

288
00:18:19,968 --> 00:18:22,387
His father
was a German Jew who...

289
00:18:22,512 --> 00:18:23,847
survived the Holocaust

290
00:18:23,972 --> 00:18:26,474
only to have the bank steal
the family money.

291
00:18:26,683 --> 00:18:28,518
And did anyone other than you
and Julian

292
00:18:28,643 --> 00:18:29,769
know about the shoulder pouch?

293
00:18:29,894 --> 00:18:31,521
We're the only ones he trusted.

294
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:36,026
Melaina, are you a tea drinker?

295
00:18:37,861 --> 00:18:38,987
I am.

296
00:18:39,946 --> 00:18:42,616
We found a toxic substance
inside your teapot.

297
00:18:44,367 --> 00:18:46,244
I don't know anything about that.

298
00:18:47,996 --> 00:18:49,914
Excuse me for asking, but...

299
00:18:50,040 --> 00:18:52,542
can anyone confirm that you
were in Big Sur last night?

300
00:18:52,709 --> 00:18:53,835
No.

301
00:18:53,960 --> 00:18:56,588
I rent a remote cabin for solitude.

302
00:18:56,713 --> 00:18:58,548
Would you consent
to a forensics exam

303
00:18:58,673 --> 00:19:00,634
and an examination
of your vehicle?

304
00:19:02,761 --> 00:19:04,012
No.

305
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:08,266
I mean, go ahead.
Do whatever you need to.

306
00:19:22,739 --> 00:19:24,115
You're not real.

307
00:19:26,076 --> 00:19:28,536
You're an hallucination.

308
00:19:30,038 --> 00:19:31,581
Depends on your point
of view, I suppose.

309
00:19:31,706 --> 00:19:33,291
It's the only reality that I know.

310
00:19:34,417 --> 00:19:36,544
How come I'm strapped down?

311
00:19:36,670 --> 00:19:38,213
'Cause you are strapped down.

312
00:19:38,797 --> 00:19:40,924
Yeah, they're pumping
your stomach right now.

313
00:19:41,466 --> 00:19:42,676
Listen.

314
00:19:42,801 --> 00:19:44,552
"Set suction at 125 intermittent."

315
00:19:44,678 --> 00:19:47,347
"Check his I.V. and get an
electrolyte bag, just in case."

316
00:19:47,472 --> 00:19:48,682
"Yes, doctor."

317
00:19:49,099 --> 00:19:50,684
Well, how about that?

318
00:19:52,394 --> 00:19:53,728
So...

319
00:19:55,897 --> 00:19:57,357
you're my daughter.

320
00:19:58,817 --> 00:20:02,112
Well, at least how I imagine
my daughter to be all grown up.

321
00:20:02,946 --> 00:20:04,864
Do I meet your approval?

322
00:20:06,408 --> 00:20:07,742
You're a bit lippy.

323
00:20:09,703 --> 00:20:11,204
Yeah, I get that from you.

324
00:20:12,622 --> 00:20:14,916
Yeah. Sorry.

325
00:20:15,166 --> 00:20:16,876
Not really crazy about that.

326
00:20:17,711 --> 00:20:20,380
Hey, you didn't exactly turn out
the way I expected, either.

327
00:20:21,005 --> 00:20:23,007
What's that supposed to mean?

328
00:20:23,717 --> 00:20:25,427
What happened to you?

329
00:20:26,636 --> 00:20:29,389
I've been dead for ten years
and you're still stuck in neutral?

330
00:20:29,931 --> 00:20:31,933
You have no life,
just this endless obsession.

331
00:20:32,058 --> 00:20:33,852
Red John. Red John.

332
00:20:35,395 --> 00:20:37,021
Mom would not be happy.

333
00:20:37,647 --> 00:20:40,400
Well, my relationship
with your mother is, uh...

334
00:20:41,526 --> 00:20:43,194
it's my business.

335
00:20:44,154 --> 00:20:45,864
Show a little respect.

336
00:20:46,489 --> 00:20:48,616
Hello, I'm a figment
of your imagination.

337
00:20:48,742 --> 00:20:50,201
Show yourself some respect.

338
00:20:50,326 --> 00:20:53,121
This is what I've been missing out
on for all these years?

339
00:20:53,329 --> 00:20:55,039
When do you go
to college again?

340
00:20:55,540 --> 00:20:58,209
Look at you. You can't even be real
with someone who doesn't even exist.

341
00:21:00,587 --> 00:21:02,464
Does anyone know
who you are?

342
00:21:02,756 --> 00:21:04,340
Yes.

343
00:21:04,466 --> 00:21:05,759
Who?

344
00:21:07,969 --> 00:21:09,637
Well, come on. Who?

345
00:21:14,142 --> 00:21:15,477
Lisbon.

346
00:21:17,896 --> 00:21:19,481
Lisbon.

347
00:21:19,606 --> 00:21:21,024
Lisbon.

348
00:21:23,026 --> 00:21:24,778
Lisbon.

349
00:21:24,903 --> 00:21:26,196
Hey.

350
00:21:26,905 --> 00:21:28,448
Hey, Jane.

351
00:21:28,573 --> 00:21:30,784
- Hey, I'm back.
- Yeah.

352
00:21:31,075 --> 00:21:32,994
You're gonna be okay.

353
00:21:33,244 --> 00:21:35,038
They pumped your stomach.

354
00:21:35,163 --> 00:21:37,248
- You took some kind of...
- Hallucinogen.

355
00:21:38,500 --> 00:21:40,126
Hallucinogen.

356
00:21:42,295 --> 00:21:44,088
Was it in the tea?

357
00:21:44,380 --> 00:21:46,299
Belladonna leaves.

358
00:21:46,883 --> 00:21:50,178
The doctor says it could take
a few hours to leave your system.

359
00:21:50,804 --> 00:21:54,098
You're gonna be okay, though.
You just need some rest.

360
00:21:54,265 --> 00:21:55,725
Yeah.

361
00:21:57,560 --> 00:21:59,187
How you feeling?

362
00:22:00,104 --> 00:22:02,190
Parched. Really...

363
00:22:04,025 --> 00:22:05,568
so thirsty.

364
00:22:06,402 --> 00:22:08,154
Can you get me a little water?

365
00:22:09,239 --> 00:22:11,282
Please, belladonna?

366
00:22:11,699 --> 00:22:14,160
- I'll be right back.
- Okay. Bye-bye.

367
00:22:33,096 --> 00:22:34,430
Ah, Jane.

368
00:22:48,367 --> 00:22:49,618
Great park.

369
00:23:10,889 --> 00:23:12,391
You're still here.

370
00:23:12,516 --> 00:23:13,975
You're still tripping.

371
00:23:16,561 --> 00:23:18,522
It would seem so.

372
00:23:18,730 --> 00:23:20,524
You really shouldn't be driving.

373
00:23:20,649 --> 00:23:23,443
And you especially should not
be driving an ambulance.

374
00:23:26,571 --> 00:23:28,573
Oh, that's some strong tea.

375
00:23:34,705 --> 00:23:36,748
Now you're stealing
an ambulance?

376
00:23:36,873 --> 00:23:39,584
No. No, I'm just admiring
the interior.

377
00:23:42,045 --> 00:23:43,922
Wicked G.P.S.

378
00:23:44,965 --> 00:23:46,800
How much you think
one of these rigs goes for?

379
00:23:46,925 --> 00:23:48,510
You need bed rest.

380
00:23:48,635 --> 00:23:50,345
I need to go back
to the crime scene.

381
00:23:51,471 --> 00:23:53,849
What's her story?
She have a boyfriend?

382
00:23:53,974 --> 00:23:55,392
Be quiet.

383
00:23:55,517 --> 00:23:58,854
- I didn't say anything.
- I was talking to the hallucination.

384
00:23:59,813 --> 00:24:02,023
- In bed now.
- When I was in the victim's kitchen

385
00:24:02,149 --> 00:24:04,234
I made a breakthrough
in the case.

386
00:24:04,735 --> 00:24:06,236
At least I thought I did.

387
00:24:07,654 --> 00:24:09,823
That's about when the wacky tea
kicked in.

388
00:24:09,990 --> 00:24:12,784
Fine. I will have Van Pelt
come down here

389
00:24:12,909 --> 00:24:14,369
with crime scene photos.

390
00:24:14,494 --> 00:24:16,246
Not the same.
I have to be there.

391
00:24:16,371 --> 00:24:18,457
Hallucinations are part
of the subconscious.

392
00:24:18,582 --> 00:24:21,126
- Mine could be clues.
- We'll go when you're lucid.

393
00:24:21,251 --> 00:24:23,336
Well, when I'm lucid,
I could forget everything.

394
00:24:23,462 --> 00:24:25,797
But right now, let me tell you...

395
00:24:25,964 --> 00:24:29,509
right now my visions
are very, very real!

396
00:24:29,926 --> 00:24:33,054
In other words, you wanna use
drugs to solve a case.

397
00:24:33,180 --> 00:24:35,724
Some great minds did their best
work on hallucinogens.

398
00:24:36,308 --> 00:24:38,143
And now you have a great mind?

399
00:24:38,477 --> 00:24:40,979
- I like her.
- Oh, yeah, funny. Double act, huh?

400
00:24:41,396 --> 00:24:43,815
Jane. You're scaring me.

401
00:24:43,940 --> 00:24:47,235
Teresa, you can call me Patrick.

402
00:24:48,445 --> 00:24:50,781
Drugs is not the path
I would choose

403
00:24:50,906 --> 00:24:53,575
but it's been chosen for me,
so I must stay the course.

404
00:24:53,700 --> 00:24:55,410
Please, I implore you

405
00:24:55,535 --> 00:24:57,329
take me back
to the crime scene.

406
00:25:00,207 --> 00:25:03,460
The Maharaja of Jodhpur
owns the Blue Orchid.

407
00:25:03,585 --> 00:25:06,880
His royal highness called personally
to inquire about my services.

408
00:25:07,005 --> 00:25:08,590
The commission was mine...

409
00:25:08,715 --> 00:25:11,551
until Viktor Mendelssohn used
his flashy credentials

410
00:25:11,676 --> 00:25:13,220
to steal my job.

411
00:25:13,720 --> 00:25:15,806
I was told you threatened Viktor.

412
00:25:15,931 --> 00:25:18,183
- I did.
- Did you kill him, too?

413
00:25:21,269 --> 00:25:22,437
Come again?

414
00:25:22,604 --> 00:25:25,148
It means "barking dogs seldom bite".

415
00:25:25,273 --> 00:25:26,942
If I intended to kill Viktor

416
00:25:27,067 --> 00:25:28,944
I wouldn't broadcast
my intentions.

417
00:25:29,069 --> 00:25:31,238
Well, things happen
in the heat of the moment.

418
00:25:31,363 --> 00:25:32,781
Things we can't control.

419
00:25:33,281 --> 00:25:35,742
Right before he was murdered
Viktor was overheard in his house

420
00:25:35,867 --> 00:25:37,744
arguing with a man
about the Blue Orchid.

421
00:25:37,869 --> 00:25:39,830
What can I say?
It wasn't me.

422
00:25:39,955 --> 00:25:43,834
But you're a barking dog, Fahad.
You just said so yourself.

423
00:25:43,959 --> 00:25:45,877
Were you barking
at Victor last night?

424
00:25:46,211 --> 00:25:47,879
What do you want from me?

425
00:25:48,004 --> 00:25:49,756
Do you want to arrest me?
Then arrest me!

426
00:25:49,881 --> 00:25:53,051
If not, I'm going back to work!
What do you have to say to that?

427
00:25:53,260 --> 00:25:55,053
You're a person of interest, Fahad.

428
00:25:55,178 --> 00:25:57,138
Don't leave town
without notifying CBI.

429
00:26:02,602 --> 00:26:04,521
Did that sound like the man
you heard yelling last night?

430
00:26:04,646 --> 00:26:05,939
It might.

431
00:26:06,147 --> 00:26:08,066
No, he didn't sound...

432
00:26:08,233 --> 00:26:09,568
I don't think that was him.

433
00:26:09,693 --> 00:26:13,321
The man I heard yelling
didn't have an accent like that.

434
00:26:18,869 --> 00:26:22,163
Last thing I remember was admiring
this beautiful butterfly.

435
00:26:22,747 --> 00:26:24,207
Right here.

436
00:26:24,332 --> 00:26:25,834
Real or imagined?

437
00:26:25,959 --> 00:26:27,544
Real, I think.

438
00:26:28,169 --> 00:26:30,130
Then I heard a bang

439
00:26:30,255 --> 00:26:32,674
in that pot there on the stove.

440
00:26:33,592 --> 00:26:35,176
What was in the pot?

441
00:26:37,053 --> 00:26:38,430
A rabbit.

442
00:26:40,724 --> 00:26:42,684
Like that rabbit?

443
00:26:45,312 --> 00:26:47,355
Yeah, just like that

444
00:26:47,480 --> 00:26:50,817
but my rabbit was real,
with a diamond necklace.

445
00:26:50,942 --> 00:26:52,527
That is some hallucinogen.

446
00:26:52,944 --> 00:26:54,529
She's kinda cute.

447
00:26:54,654 --> 00:26:56,615
- I mean, have you guys ever...
- You be quiet.

448
00:26:58,241 --> 00:26:59,367
Pressing on

449
00:26:59,492 --> 00:27:01,912
I carried the rabbit
to the foyer...

450
00:27:03,830 --> 00:27:05,582
and it jumped from my hands

451
00:27:05,790 --> 00:27:07,792
and hopped out the door.

452
00:27:07,918 --> 00:27:11,046
Then I moved down
the walkway looking.

453
00:27:11,212 --> 00:27:14,341
"Where's the bunny?
Here, little bunny, bunny."

454
00:27:14,549 --> 00:27:18,261
And I went through
the gawking neighbors'

455
00:27:18,762 --> 00:27:21,014
and across the street.

456
00:27:23,266 --> 00:27:26,686
Right here, I met this lovely
Scandinavian couple

457
00:27:26,811 --> 00:27:30,357
that spotted the rabbit
hopping up that path.

458
00:27:30,482 --> 00:27:32,567
- They were Scandinavian?
- Yes.

459
00:27:33,234 --> 00:27:34,945
It's a clue, right?

460
00:27:35,111 --> 00:27:38,031
Scandinavia. Reindeer.

461
00:27:38,239 --> 00:27:40,116
Reindeer, Christmas.

462
00:27:40,992 --> 00:27:44,245
I might have just figured out that the killer
has a December birthday.

463
00:27:49,626 --> 00:27:52,170
Did the couple you met
kinda look like them?

464
00:27:56,716 --> 00:27:59,177
You must have walked by them
on your way to the backyard.

465
00:27:59,552 --> 00:28:00,887
Classic.

466
00:28:01,596 --> 00:28:04,808
Still waiting for the subconscious
clues to kick in, Jane.

467
00:28:04,933 --> 00:28:06,893
They will. Follow me.

468
00:28:18,613 --> 00:28:21,157
- This is wrong.
- What's wrong?

469
00:28:22,450 --> 00:28:24,119
This yard's different.

470
00:28:24,995 --> 00:28:27,455
In my hallucination,
this was a...

471
00:28:28,415 --> 00:28:30,542
manicured garden with a...

472
00:28:30,667 --> 00:28:32,127
- Look at that pool!
- thing and a...

473
00:28:33,545 --> 00:28:34,587
This...

474
00:28:34,713 --> 00:28:36,715
Okay, so tell me what happened
back here in your vision.

475
00:28:37,007 --> 00:28:38,258
The water's great.

476
00:28:38,383 --> 00:28:39,592
It's where I met Charlotte.

477
00:28:39,759 --> 00:28:41,052
Charlotte?

478
00:28:41,177 --> 00:28:42,512
My daughter.

479
00:28:43,555 --> 00:28:46,141
- Is that who you've been talking to?
- Yes.

480
00:28:46,516 --> 00:28:48,226
She here right now?

481
00:28:48,685 --> 00:28:50,020
Yeah.

482
00:28:52,397 --> 00:28:53,773
Wh-what is she doing?

483
00:28:55,775 --> 00:28:57,694
She's doing the backstroke.

484
00:28:58,319 --> 00:28:59,612
I taught her that.

485
00:29:01,740 --> 00:29:04,868
Jane, maybe your subconscious

486
00:29:04,993 --> 00:29:07,829
didn't send you any clues
from the crime scene.

487
00:29:07,996 --> 00:29:09,748
Maybe it sent you
your daughter.

488
00:29:11,416 --> 00:29:13,168
- Why?
- I don't know.

489
00:29:14,210 --> 00:29:15,754
Can we go back
to the hospital now?

490
00:29:15,879 --> 00:29:17,088
- Workshop.
- What?

491
00:29:17,255 --> 00:29:19,049
Charlotte took me
to the diamond cutter's workshop.

492
00:29:19,174 --> 00:29:20,967
Last stop, I promise.

493
00:29:21,092 --> 00:29:23,428
If we don't find anything,
I will tuck myself into bed

494
00:29:23,553 --> 00:29:25,764
- and sleep it off.
- I'm gonna hold you to that.

495
00:29:44,899 --> 00:29:46,067
Hey.

496
00:29:51,823 --> 00:29:52,865
You have a key?

497
00:29:52,991 --> 00:29:54,742
Yeah, Mendelssohn's daughter
gave it to me.

498
00:29:54,867 --> 00:29:57,412
She said her father's
workshop was, uh...

499
00:29:57,537 --> 00:29:58,830
in the back here.

500
00:30:09,148 --> 00:30:10,233
And who are you?

501
00:30:10,358 --> 00:30:13,236
CBI. We're investigating
Viktor Mendelssohn's murder.

502
00:30:13,361 --> 00:30:14,529
You're his assistant?

503
00:30:14,695 --> 00:30:16,906
Julian Gallego.
Uh, I work here.

504
00:30:17,615 --> 00:30:19,283
It's not what I imagined.

505
00:30:19,534 --> 00:30:20,910
You mind
if we take a look around?

506
00:30:21,035 --> 00:30:23,246
Not at all.
I'm just packing up. Please.

507
00:30:23,371 --> 00:30:24,580
Thank you.

508
00:30:34,632 --> 00:30:36,300
I like the other workshop better.

509
00:30:40,012 --> 00:30:41,722
This is so dull.

510
00:30:41,848 --> 00:30:43,766
Why do even work
for the CBI, anyway?

511
00:30:44,809 --> 00:30:46,185
You know why.

512
00:30:46,978 --> 00:30:48,563
To catch Red John.

513
00:30:49,605 --> 00:30:51,441
Okay. Whatever.

514
00:30:54,777 --> 00:30:57,363
What does that mean?
"Okay, whatever."

515
00:30:59,824 --> 00:31:02,618
Well, to be honest, Dad your obsession's
a little creepy and weird, you know.

516
00:31:03,828 --> 00:31:05,246
What?

517
00:31:05,746 --> 00:31:08,583
I do it all for you.
And your mom.

518
00:31:09,750 --> 00:31:11,210
Yeah.

519
00:31:11,419 --> 00:31:14,172
And I hate to be the one
to break this to you, Dad but...

520
00:31:15,214 --> 00:31:17,216
there's nothing
that you can do for us.

521
00:31:18,885 --> 00:31:21,262
We don't give a damn
what happens to Red John.

522
00:31:22,638 --> 00:31:24,223
I mean, we're dead.

523
00:31:24,891 --> 00:31:26,058
Gone.

524
00:31:26,184 --> 00:31:28,352
And you need to start dealing
with that.

525
00:31:31,189 --> 00:31:33,274
I deal with that every day.

526
00:31:33,399 --> 00:31:35,276
Maybe that's the problem.

527
00:31:39,363 --> 00:31:40,907
Could you please move?

528
00:31:43,284 --> 00:31:45,077
Don't roll your eyes at me.

529
00:31:49,248 --> 00:31:52,168
Uh, you don't need to go in there.
That's, uh, just my stuff.

530
00:32:08,684 --> 00:32:11,062
You found it.
You found the Blue Orchid.

531
00:32:11,187 --> 00:32:12,939
Yes.

532
00:32:13,064 --> 00:32:14,649
In your stuff.

533
00:32:15,566 --> 00:32:19,153
What's a 20-million-dollar
missing diamond doing in your bag?

534
00:32:19,278 --> 00:32:21,948
Viktor's daughter said he kept it
on his body at all times.

535
00:32:22,073 --> 00:32:24,242
I-I don't know
how it got in there.

536
00:32:24,867 --> 00:32:27,119
Wait, you're not accusing me?

537
00:32:28,621 --> 00:32:30,540
Viktor's neighbor
said she heard a man

538
00:32:30,665 --> 00:32:34,043
arguing with Viktor last night
about a diamond.

539
00:32:34,168 --> 00:32:35,836
Was that man you, Julian?

540
00:32:35,962 --> 00:32:37,505
No.

541
00:32:37,630 --> 00:32:41,008
I mean... Okay, yes.
Um, I was there.

542
00:32:41,133 --> 00:32:42,843
- So you lied to Agent Van Pelt?
- No.

543
00:32:42,969 --> 00:32:45,680
She never directly asked me
if I was at Viktor's house last night.

544
00:32:45,805 --> 00:32:47,515
- You should've told her.
- How would that have sounded?

545
00:32:47,682 --> 00:32:49,392
That you fought
with your boss, drugged him

546
00:32:49,517 --> 00:32:51,269
stole his client's diamonds,
and then killed him.

547
00:32:52,728 --> 00:32:55,064
We had a creative argument
over how to shape this stone.

548
00:32:55,189 --> 00:32:58,109
We're both artists, passionate.
I didn't kill Viktor.

549
00:32:58,401 --> 00:32:59,569
I idolized him.

550
00:32:59,694 --> 00:33:01,279
Turn around and put your hands
behind your head.

551
00:33:01,404 --> 00:33:03,030
- What?
- Do it.

552
00:33:03,364 --> 00:33:04,407
Hey!

553
00:33:04,991 --> 00:33:06,158
Come here.

554
00:33:08,077 --> 00:33:09,579
Well, that certainly was easy.

555
00:33:09,745 --> 00:33:12,415
Yeah, you're just saying that
because I found the diamond.

556
00:33:13,249 --> 00:33:14,458
You wish.

557
00:33:15,251 --> 00:33:16,586
Come on.

558
00:33:16,794 --> 00:33:18,796
All righty then.
It's been real.

559
00:33:19,297 --> 00:33:21,465
Hang on. Where...
where are you going?

560
00:33:21,716 --> 00:33:23,009
Hello, I'm practically
a grown woman.

561
00:33:23,134 --> 00:33:25,094
I don't need to tell you
where I'm going.

562
00:33:25,928 --> 00:33:28,306
No, yeah, I...
I-I know, but...

563
00:33:29,473 --> 00:33:31,225
You're coming back.

564
00:33:33,853 --> 00:33:35,313
I love you, Dad.

565
00:33:37,648 --> 00:33:39,483
- Hey, Jane?
- No, not now.

566
00:33:42,820 --> 00:33:43,988
Wait.

567
00:33:59,967 --> 00:34:02,553
Julian's in holding.
The D.A. likes the case.

568
00:34:03,888 --> 00:34:06,766
- Is Charlotte...
- Gone. Tea wore off.

569
00:34:07,558 --> 00:34:10,186
Well, you were right.

570
00:34:10,311 --> 00:34:12,355
Your hallucination led us
to the killer.

571
00:34:12,521 --> 00:34:14,815
No, my imaginary daughter
was right.

572
00:34:14,982 --> 00:34:16,442
It's all too easy.

573
00:34:17,318 --> 00:34:19,028
What are you talking about?

574
00:34:19,570 --> 00:34:23,032
Well, I've never been to that workshop.
I've never even met the apprentice.

575
00:34:23,157 --> 00:34:25,409
How would I know he was stashing
stolen goods there?

576
00:34:25,534 --> 00:34:27,078
Who cares?
You found the rock.

577
00:34:27,244 --> 00:34:28,829
That doesn't mean
we found the killer.

578
00:34:28,996 --> 00:34:30,289
We've got a match
for the second set of prints

579
00:34:30,414 --> 00:34:32,500
on the murder weapon.
They belong to Julian.

580
00:34:33,084 --> 00:34:34,710
Meh, you coppers
and your evidence.

581
00:34:34,835 --> 00:34:37,046
You always let it
confuse everything.

582
00:34:37,296 --> 00:34:39,507
Of course Julian's prints
are gonna be on his boss' tools.

583
00:34:39,632 --> 00:34:41,676
They were master
and apprentice, right?

584
00:34:41,801 --> 00:34:43,094
You're really serious about this?

585
00:34:43,219 --> 00:34:45,888
Yes. There's something
I am not seeing.

586
00:34:48,391 --> 00:34:50,101
Rigsby, where's that necklace?

587
00:34:50,226 --> 00:34:51,435
What necklace?

588
00:34:51,560 --> 00:34:53,938
The one I handed you from
the maid that found the body.

589
00:34:54,772 --> 00:34:56,482
The gardener found the body.

590
00:34:56,732 --> 00:34:57,900
Well...

591
00:34:58,651 --> 00:35:00,653
That means I hallucinated the maid.

592
00:35:00,987 --> 00:35:02,446
Why?

593
00:35:02,613 --> 00:35:04,407
Send myself a message.

594
00:35:04,615 --> 00:35:06,617
An eagle claw necklace.

595
00:35:06,784 --> 00:35:08,786
What are eagles?
Don't answer that.

596
00:35:08,953 --> 00:35:12,707
They're hunters in the sky
with excellent vision.

597
00:35:16,460 --> 00:35:19,672
Just after I... poured the tea

598
00:35:19,839 --> 00:35:22,049
I was looking through
the kitchen window

599
00:35:22,591 --> 00:35:26,470
watching a butterfly
and I saw the bedroom

600
00:35:26,595 --> 00:35:29,473
of that, uh, neighbor lady...
what was her name?

601
00:35:29,598 --> 00:35:31,308
- Betty.
- Betty. Yes.

602
00:35:31,434 --> 00:35:32,852
Her bedroom
was on the second floor

603
00:35:32,977 --> 00:35:35,771
up high, with an eagle's view.

604
00:35:35,896 --> 00:35:37,148
How do you know
it was her bedroom?

605
00:35:37,273 --> 00:35:39,650
She had lace curtains
and blackout shades.

606
00:35:39,817 --> 00:35:41,318
Betty told me
she couldn't see the kitchen

607
00:35:41,485 --> 00:35:42,862
from her bedroom window.

608
00:35:43,029 --> 00:35:44,488
She lied.

609
00:35:52,455 --> 00:35:54,165
- Okay. Play it cool.
- Okay.

610
00:35:57,293 --> 00:35:58,419
Hi!

611
00:35:59,128 --> 00:36:01,547
How's the, uh, investigation going?

612
00:36:03,257 --> 00:36:05,760
Well, to tell you the truth, uh...

613
00:36:06,469 --> 00:36:08,012
we're a little stuck.

614
00:36:08,137 --> 00:36:11,182
Oh. Well, if I can be
of any more help...

615
00:36:11,307 --> 00:36:12,683
There's an idea.

616
00:36:13,809 --> 00:36:16,353
Oh, uh, uh, would you like
to come inside?

617
00:36:16,520 --> 00:36:18,272
Only if there's a cup
of tea involved.

618
00:36:18,689 --> 00:36:20,191
I'd love a cup of tea.

619
00:36:20,357 --> 00:36:23,027
- Sure. Come on.
- Oh, fantastic.

620
00:36:26,447 --> 00:36:28,157
We're closing in on Viktor's killer

621
00:36:28,324 --> 00:36:29,658
but before we make an arrest

622
00:36:29,784 --> 00:36:31,410
we need to find
his client's diamonds.

623
00:36:31,577 --> 00:36:34,497
The Blue Orchid. I've been following
the news all day.

624
00:36:34,705 --> 00:36:36,165
Oh, no, w-we found that.

625
00:36:36,332 --> 00:36:39,585
The... the Blue Orchid was hidden
in the, uh, bag of Viktor's apprentice.

626
00:36:39,710 --> 00:36:42,922
- Oh, so you have your killer?
- No. We don't think so.

627
00:36:44,256 --> 00:36:46,509
Uh, relax, I'll fix the tea.

628
00:36:46,717 --> 00:36:49,929
Only a foolish killer
would keep the Blue Orchid.

629
00:36:50,179 --> 00:36:53,224
It's hard to move such
a high-profile piece quietly.

630
00:36:53,349 --> 00:36:54,600
Oh, I see.

631
00:36:54,725 --> 00:36:56,977
A smart killer would use
the Blue Orchid

632
00:36:57,103 --> 00:36:59,313
to frame an innocent man
for murder

633
00:36:59,438 --> 00:37:00,898
and keep the small diamonds.

634
00:37:01,065 --> 00:37:03,734
They're easier to fence
but still worth a couple million.

635
00:37:03,859 --> 00:37:06,320
Does anyone want, uh, lemon
with their tea?

636
00:37:07,279 --> 00:37:09,990
- Just, uh, top that up there.
- Thank you.

637
00:37:10,783 --> 00:37:13,244
There's... there's just one thing
I don't understand.

638
00:37:13,410 --> 00:37:17,039
Why don't you believe
that Julian killed Viktor?

639
00:37:17,164 --> 00:37:18,624
Doesn't that make more sense

640
00:37:18,791 --> 00:37:21,836
it's more plausible,
than somebody framing Julian?

641
00:37:21,961 --> 00:37:23,379
Framing him was the easy part.

642
00:37:23,546 --> 00:37:25,464
After Viktor was dead,
all the killer had to do

643
00:37:25,631 --> 00:37:27,925
was take his keys,
drive to his workshop

644
00:37:28,092 --> 00:37:30,970
and plant the Blue Orchid
in Julian's bag.

645
00:37:31,470 --> 00:37:34,515
Well, if that was the easy part
what was the hard part?

646
00:37:34,640 --> 00:37:36,934
Uh, gutting Viktor like a fish.

647
00:37:37,184 --> 00:37:38,602
Oh, dear.

648
00:37:39,520 --> 00:37:41,188
- What's wrong?
- Oh, my goodness!

649
00:37:41,355 --> 00:37:43,399
Something is tickling
my toe... oh, there it...

650
00:37:44,733 --> 00:37:46,402
Where did that... oh!

651
00:37:47,444 --> 00:37:49,071
Where did that come from?

652
00:37:49,196 --> 00:37:51,323
Uh, where did what
come from?

653
00:37:51,740 --> 00:37:53,701
- The rabbit.
- What rabbit?

654
00:37:53,868 --> 00:37:55,077
I have no idea.

655
00:37:55,244 --> 00:37:56,787
The... the rabbit!

656
00:37:56,912 --> 00:37:58,914
There's a rabbit right here
in my house!

657
00:37:59,081 --> 00:38:00,583
Where did it come from?

658
00:38:11,719 --> 00:38:14,471
What is wrong with you people?
Why are...

659
00:38:15,431 --> 00:38:17,391
Oh, no.
Oh, my Lord.

660
00:38:17,516 --> 00:38:19,435
Which... which tea did you serve me?

661
00:38:20,394 --> 00:38:21,854
Oh, Lord. Oh, Lord.

662
00:38:21,979 --> 00:38:24,106
Oh, Lord. Oh, my God.
No, no, no, no, no, no!

663
00:38:24,273 --> 00:38:25,566
No, no, no.

664
00:38:25,816 --> 00:38:29,528
I will not answer your questions.
I will not answer anything.

665
00:38:29,653 --> 00:38:31,655
I will not tell you
what you wanna know.

666
00:38:32,489 --> 00:38:33,532
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa,
whoa, whoa, whoa.

667
00:38:33,699 --> 00:38:35,075
No. No, please.
Let me go.

668
00:38:35,201 --> 00:38:36,327
- Let me go!
- What do you got there?

669
00:38:36,452 --> 00:38:38,078
No! No, I need help!

670
00:38:38,412 --> 00:38:39,455
- Please!
-Viktor's shoulder pouch.

671
00:38:39,580 --> 00:38:42,291
No, I need medical help! Please!
I just drank some belladonna!

672
00:38:42,416 --> 00:38:44,376
- I need help!
- You drank raspberry hibiscus.

673
00:38:44,543 --> 00:38:46,712
Sweet like belladonna,
it doesn't pack the punch.

674
00:38:46,837 --> 00:38:48,255
Believe me.

675
00:38:48,380 --> 00:38:50,507
But the... but the...
the... the rabbit...

676
00:38:51,091 --> 00:38:52,551
Local pet store.

677
00:38:54,011 --> 00:38:55,346
You tricked me.

678
00:38:59,683 --> 00:39:01,727
- Come on. Let's go.
- You tricked me.

679
00:39:06,815 --> 00:39:09,068
Your finances are a mess, Betty.

680
00:39:09,193 --> 00:39:12,112
The money from your late husband's
insurance policy, long gone.

681
00:39:12,279 --> 00:39:13,948
You couldn't make
next month's rent.

682
00:39:14,114 --> 00:39:15,282
2 million in stolen diamonds

683
00:39:15,449 --> 00:39:18,118
would've kept you living
in style for a long time.

684
00:39:18,285 --> 00:39:20,246
What was he thinking?

685
00:39:20,412 --> 00:39:23,749
An old man walking around
with all those jewels?

686
00:39:24,291 --> 00:39:26,502
He was begging for it.

687
00:39:26,627 --> 00:39:27,920
How did you know
he carried them on him?

688
00:39:28,087 --> 00:39:29,672
Oh, please,
I've known for years.

689
00:39:29,838 --> 00:39:32,925
His... his... his kitchen window's right
across from my bedroom.

690
00:39:33,092 --> 00:39:35,427
Every night
I would lie in bed thinking...

691
00:39:35,886 --> 00:39:38,514
"How can I get my hands
on that pouch?"

692
00:39:38,681 --> 00:39:39,932
The Blue Orchid
was your shot?

693
00:39:40,057 --> 00:39:44,520
Oh, framing Julian was one of, like
a dozen plans I've had over the years.

694
00:39:44,645 --> 00:39:46,146
The daughter was at the cabin

695
00:39:46,272 --> 00:39:49,483
and when I saw the two of them
in the kitchen with that big rock

696
00:39:50,067 --> 00:39:51,819
I knew it was now or never.

697
00:39:51,986 --> 00:39:55,739
Once Julian left,
I just... I went on autopilot.

698
00:39:56,949 --> 00:39:58,284
Where'd you get
the belladonna?

699
00:39:58,450 --> 00:40:00,869
I've had it for months
for just such an occasion.

700
00:40:00,995 --> 00:40:04,123
All I had to do was drop in
and offer him a pot of tea.

701
00:40:05,374 --> 00:40:07,960
The hallucinations
made it easier to kill him?

702
00:40:08,085 --> 00:40:10,087
That was the original plan.

703
00:40:10,212 --> 00:40:12,673
But once I lifted
the diamonds off Viktor

704
00:40:12,840 --> 00:40:15,926
it... it... it was
the craziest thing.

705
00:40:16,051 --> 00:40:18,387
He became so delusional

706
00:40:18,554 --> 00:40:21,098
I convinced him
to take his own life.

707
00:40:21,890 --> 00:40:24,184
What do you mean?
How?

708
00:40:25,227 --> 00:40:30,107
I told Viktor that the diamonds
were trapped in his stomach.

